distances behind each other when they land. A joint <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NASA\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:NASA\">NASA</a>-FAA program aimed at boosting <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/airport\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:airport\">airport</a> capacity, however, is aimed at determining conditions under which <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/airplane\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:airplane\">planes</a> may fly closer together. NASA researchers are studying wake vortex with a variety of tools, from <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/supercomputer\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:supercomputer\">supercomputers</a>, to <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/wind_tunnel\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:wind tunnel\">wind tunnels</a>, to actual flight tests in research aircraft. Their goal is to fully understand the phenomenon, then use that knowledge to create an automated system that could predict changing wake vortex conditions at airports. <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aviator\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:Aviator\">Pilots</a> already know, for example, that they have to worry less about wake vortex in rough weather because windy conditions cause them to dissipate more rapidly.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"This image or video was catalogued by Langley Research Center of the United States <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NASA\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"w:NASA\">National Aeronautics and Space Administration</a> (NASA)\u00a0under <b>Photo\u00a0ID:</b> <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://images.nasa.gov/search?q=EL-1996-00130&amp;page=1&amp;media=image,video,audio&amp;yearStart=1920&amp;yearEnd=2026\">EL-1996-00130</a>\u00a0and <b>Alternate\u00a0ID:</b> <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://images.nasa.gov/search?q=L90-5919&amp;page=1&amp;media=image,video,audio&amp;yearStart=1920&amp;yearEnd=2026\">L90-5919</a>.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" data-mw-original-href=\"http://lisar.larc.nasa.gov/\" href=\"https://lisar.larc.nasa.gov/\">NASA Langley Research Center (NASA-LaRC)</a>, Edited by <a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/User:Fir0002\" title=\"User:Fir0002\">Fir0002</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"UsageTerms":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Restrictions":{"value":"","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}}}]},"1756063":{"pageid":1756063,"ns":6,"title":"File:Airplane vortex edit2.jpg","index":7,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":553361,"width":1800,"height":1400,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Airplane_vortex_edit2.jpg/500px-Airplane_vortex_edit2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":500,"thumbheight":389,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Airplane_vortex_edit2.jpg/1280px-Airplane_vortex_edit2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Airplane_vortex_edit2.jpg/500px-Airplane_vortex_edit2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":500,"height":389,"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Airplane_vortex_edit2.jpg/1280px-Airplane_vortex_edit2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/5d/Airplane_vortex_edit2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Airplane_vortex_edit2.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=1756063","extmetadata":{"ImageDescription":{"value":"Wake Vortex Study at <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wallops_Flight_Facility\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"w:Wallops Flight Facility\">Wallops Flight Facility</a> <br>\nThe air flow from the wing of this agricultural plane is made visible by a technique that uses colored smoke rising from the ground. The swirl at the wingtip traces the aircraft's <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/wake_vortex\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"w:wake vortex\">wake vortex</a>, which exerts a powerful influence on the flow field behind the plane. Because of wake vortex, the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_Aviation_Administration\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"w:Federal Aviation Administration\">Federal Aviation Administration</a> (FAA) requires aircraft to maintain set distances behind each other when they land. A joint <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NASA\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"w:NASA\">NASA</a>-FAA program aimed at boosting airport capacity, however, is aimed at determining conditions under which planes may fly closer together. NASA researchers are studying wake vortex with a variety of tools, from supercomputers to wind tunnels to actual flight tests in research aircraft. Their goal is to fully understand the phenomenon, then use that knowledge to create an automated system that could predict changing wake vortex conditions at airports. Pilots already know, for example, that they have to worry less about wake vortex in rough weather because windy conditions cause them to dissipate more rapidly.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"This file was derived from:  <a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Airplane_vortex_edit.jpg\" title=\"File:Airplane vortex edit.jpg\">Airplane vortex edit.jpg</a><br>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"NASA / <a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/User:Fir0002\" title=\"User:Fir0002\">Fir0002</a> / <a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/User:Trialsanderrors\" title=\"User:Trialsanderrors\">Trialsanderrors</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"UsageTerms":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Restrictions":{"value":"","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}}}]},"189501685":{"pageid":189501685,"ns":6,"title":"File:B-727 in flight during vortex study with wingtip smoke generators ECN-3831.jpg","index":2,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":345663,"width":2000,"height":1720,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/B-727_in_flight_during_vortex_study_with_wingtip_smoke_generators_ECN-3831.jpg/500px-B-727_in_flight_during_vortex_study_with_wingtip_smoke_generators_ECN-3831.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":500,"thumbheight":430,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/B-727_in_flight_during_vortex_study_with_wingtip_smoke_generators_ECN-3831.jpg/1280px-B-727_in_flight_during_vortex_study_with_wingtip_smoke_generators_ECN-3831.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/B-727_in_flight_during_vortex_study_with_wingtip_smoke_generators_ECN-3831.jpg/500px-B-727_in_flight_during_vortex_study_with_wingtip_smoke_generators_ECN-3831.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":500,"height":430,"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0b/B-727_in_flight_during_vortex_study_with_wingtip_smoke_generators_ECN-3831.jpg/1280px-B-727_in_flight_during_vortex_study_with_wingtip_smoke_generators_ECN-3831.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0b/B-727_in_flight_during_vortex_study_with_wingtip_smoke_generators_ECN-3831.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:B-727_in_flight_during_vortex_study_with_wingtip_smoke_generators_ECN-3831.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=189501685","extmetadata":{"ImageDescription":{"value":"Photo\n<p>Description: A Boeing 727 jetliner is shown in this 1974 NASA Flight Research Center photograph taking part in a wake vortex study. Smoke from \u201csmoke generators\u201d installed on the tips of the aircraft\u2019s wings is shown being entrained by the wing tip vortices. A Piper PA-30 Twin Comanche and/or an F-104 or Lear Jet model 23 would fly behind the 727 to measure the effects of the airliner's wake vortices during landing approaches.\n</p><p>Project\n</p>\nDescription: A series of flight tests using a Boeing 727 jetliner was conducted in 1973-1974 at the NASA Flight Research Center (FRC), which became the Dryden Flight Research Center in 1976, to study the effects of wake vortices emanating from the trailing edges of the wings. The NASA study in conjunction with the Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) was in response to the hazards being encountered when small aircraft trailing behind new large jumbojets experienced \u201cupset\u201d problems.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://web.archive.org/web/20090403132316/http://www.dfrc.nasa.gov/Gallery/Photo/B-727/HTML/ECN-3831.html\">https://web.archive.org/web/20090403132316/http://www.dfrc.nasa.gov/Gallery/Photo/B-727/HTML/ECN-3831.html</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"NASA/Dryden Flight Research Center","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"UsageTerms":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Restrictions":{"value":"","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}}}]},"187948670":{"pageid":187948670,"ns":6,"title":"File:Boeing 747 ECN-11848A.jpg","index":12,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":4486254,"width":3000,"height":2631,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Boeing_747_ECN-11848A.jpg/500px-Boeing_747_ECN-11848A.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":500,"thumbheight":439,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Boeing_747_ECN-11848A.jpg/1280px-Boeing_747_ECN-11848A.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Boeing_747_ECN-11848A.jpg/500px-Boeing_747_ECN-11848A.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":500,"height":439,"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Boeing_747_ECN-11848A.jpg/1280px-Boeing_747_ECN-11848A.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/db/Boeing_747_ECN-11848A.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boeing_747_ECN-11848A.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=187948670","extmetadata":{"ImageDescription":{"value":"Photo Description: The Boeing 747 used for wingtip vortex research flights sits on the ramp at NASA\u2019s Flight Research Center in Edwards, California. Note the smoke generator mounted underneath the jet\u2019s wing. Smoke from underwing smoke generators made it possible for researchers to actually see the vortices created by the 747\u2019s wings in flight.\n<p>Project\nDescription: In 1974 the NASA Flight Research Center (later Dryden Flight Research Center, Edwards, California) used a Boeing 747 as part of the overall NASA study of trailing vortices. Trailing vortices are the invisible flow of spiraling air that trails from the wings of large aircraft and can \"upset\" smaller aircraft flying behind them. The 747 that NASA used was on loan from the Johnson Space Center where it was part of the Space Shuttle Program.\nThe data gathered in the 747 studies complemented data from the previous (1973-74) joint NASA Flight Research Center and Federal Aviation Administration (FAA) Boeing 727 wake vortices study.\n</p><p>Six smoke generators were installed under the wings of the 747 to provide a visual image of the trailing vortices. The object of the experiments was to test different configurations and mechanical devices on the747 that could be used to break up or lessen the strength of the vortices. The results of the tests could lead to shorter spacing between landings and takeoffs, which, in turn, could alleviate air-traffic congestion. For approximately 30 flights the 747 was flown using various combinations of wing air spoilers in an attempt to reduce wake vortices. To evaluate the effectiveness of the different configurations, chase aircraft were flown into the vortex sheets to probe their strengths and patterns at different times. Two of the chase planes used were the Flight Research Center\u2019s Cessna T-37 and the NASA Ames Research Center\u2019s Learjet. These aircraft represented the types of smaller business jets and other small aircraft that might encounter large passenger aircraft on approach or landings around major airports or in flight.\n</p>\nTests without the 747\u2019s wing spoilers deployed produced violent \"upset\" problems for the T-37 aircraft at a distance of approximately 3 miles. From the magnitude of the problems found, distances of as much as ten miles might be required if spoilers were not used. With two spoilers on the outer wing panels, the T-37 could fly at a distance of three miles and not experience the \"upset\" problem. The wake vortex study continued even after the 747 was returned to its primary mission of carrying the Space Shuttle.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://web.archive.org/web/20090407113910/http://www1.dfrc.nasa.gov/Gallery/Photo/B-747/HTML/ECN-11848A.html\">https://web.archive.org/web/20090407113910/http://www1.dfrc.nasa.gov/Gallery/Photo/B-747/HTML/ECN-11848A.html</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"NASA/Dryden Flight Research Center","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"UsageTerms":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Restrictions":{"value":"","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}}}]},"7227757":{"pageid":7227757,"ns":6,"title":"File:Boeing 747 performing wing vortex trials 2.jpg","index":9,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":8074272,"width":3030,"height":2355,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Boeing_747_performing_wing_vortex_trials_2.jpg/500px-Boeing_747_performing_wing_vortex_trials_2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":500,"thumbheight":389,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Boeing_747_performing_wing_vortex_trials_2.jpg/1280px-Boeing_747_performing_wing_vortex_trials_2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Boeing_747_performing_wing_vortex_trials_2.jpg/500px-Boeing_747_performing_wing_vortex_trials_2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":500,"height":389,"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Boeing_747_performing_wing_vortex_trials_2.jpg/1280px-Boeing_747_performing_wing_vortex_trials_2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9d/Boeing_747_performing_wing_vortex_trials_2.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boeing_747_performing_wing_vortex_trials_2.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=7227757","extmetadata":{"ImageDescription":{"value":"Two chase aircraft, a Learjet 23 (NASA 701) and a Cessna T-37 (NASA/USAF 00084), are shown in formation with a Boeing B-747 jetliner (NASA 905) in this 1974 NASA Flight Research Center (FRC) photograph. The two chase aircraft were used to probe the trailing wake vortices generated by the airflow around the wings of the B-747 aircraft. The vortex trails were made visible by smoke generators mounted under the wings of the B-747 aircraft.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" data-mw-original-href=\"http://www.dfrc.nasa.gov/Gallery/Photo/B-747/HTML/ECN-4243.html\" href=\"https://www.dfrc.nasa.gov/Gallery/Photo/B-747/HTML/ECN-4243.html\">http://www.dfrc.nasa.gov/Gallery/Photo/B-747/HTML/ECN-4243.html</a> (<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" data-mw-original-href=\"http://www.dfrc.nasa.gov/Gallery/Photo/B-747/Large/ECN-4243.jpg\" href=\"https://www.dfrc.nasa.gov/Gallery/Photo/B-747/Large/ECN-4243.jpg\">direct link</a>)","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"NASA","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"UsageTerms":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Restrictions":{"value":"","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}}}]},"6591199":{"pageid":6591199,"ns":6,"title":"File:Boeing 747 vortex study - ECN-4245.jpg","index":6,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":506467,"width":2000,"height":1586,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Boeing_747_vortex_study_-_ECN-4245.jpg/500px-Boeing_747_vortex_study_-_ECN-4245.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":500,"thumbheight":397,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Boeing_747_vortex_study_-_ECN-4245.jpg/1280px-Boeing_747_vortex_study_-_ECN-4245.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Boeing_747_vortex_study_-_ECN-4245.jpg/500px-Boeing_747_vortex_study_-_ECN-4245.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":500,"height":397,"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e5/Boeing_747_vortex_study_-_ECN-4245.jpg/1280px-Boeing_747_vortex_study_-_ECN-4245.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e5/Boeing_747_vortex_study_-_ECN-4245.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boeing_747_vortex_study_-_ECN-4245.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=6591199","extmetadata":{"Artist":{"value":"<div class=\"fn value\">\nNASA</div>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"ImageDescription":{"value":"<div class=\"description\">\nA Learjet 23 (NASA 701), a Cessna T-37 and a Boeing 747-100 (NASA 905; tail #N905NA)\uff1a Two chase aircraft, a Learjet and a Cessna T-37, are shown in formation with a Boeing B-747 jetliner in this 1974 NASA Flight Research Center (FRC) photograph. The two chase aircraft were used to probe the trailing wake vortices generated by the airflow around the wings of the B-747 aircraft. The vortex trails were made visible by smoke generators mounted under the wings of the B-747 aircraft.</div>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.dfrc.nasa.gov/Gallery/Photo/B-747/HTML/ECN-4245.html\">https://www.dfrc.nasa.gov/Gallery/Photo/B-747/HTML/ECN-4245.html</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"UsageTerms":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Restrictions":{"value":"","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}}}]},"1201969":{"pageid":1201969,"ns":6,"title":"File:ECN-7848a.jpg","index":11,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":522855,"width":1600,"height":1800,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/ECN-7848a.jpg/500px-ECN-7848a.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":500,"thumbheight":563,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/ECN-7848a.jpg/1280px-ECN-7848a.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/ECN-7848a.jpg/500px-ECN-7848a.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":500,"height":563,"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/ECN-7848a.jpg/1280px-ECN-7848a.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/4/4a/ECN-7848a.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ECN-7848a.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=1201969","extmetadata":{"ImageDescription":{"value":"Dryden Flight Research Center, Edwards, California, conducted extensive wake vortice tests beginning in the 1970's. These wake vortex problems first became a serious concern when large jetliners were first introduced. The aircraft's trailing vortices, created by any large-bodied aircraft, were powerful enough to cause problems for business jets and even other airliners.\n<p>Dryden became interested in vortex research both for safety and as a matter of aerodynamics. A wingtip vortex seriously reduces efficency, causing drag, and therefore a consequent penalty in fuel consumption and performance.\n</p><p>The majority of the tests were conducted using spoilers on a Boeing 747-100, which NASA had just acquired for the Space Shuttle Approach and Landing (ALT) tests. With the two spoilers on the outer panels of each wing extended, the vortices were greatly reduced and the chase aircraft could safely fly five kilometers behind the large aircraft, compared to 15 kilometers with no spoilers.\n</p><p>Dryden's 747 wake vortex studies clearly indicated that the use of spoilers could reduce the severity of wake vortices. In July, 1977, the center began a brief series of tests on a Lockheed L-1011 Tristar to determine if the spoiler that worked so well on the 747 could be applied to other wide-body aircraft as well.\n</p><p>The test showed that while spoilers on the Tristar could reduce wake vortices, they were not as effective in doing so as the spoilers on the 747. NASA is continuing wake vortex studies to this day. These projects can be expected to improve the operational safety of many future aircraft.\n</p>\nThis photo demonstrates the L-1011 with smoke generators operating, to visualize the vortex flows.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.dfrc.nasa.gov/Gallery/Photo/L-1011/HTML/ECN-7848.html\">https://www.dfrc.nasa.gov/Gallery/Photo/L-1011/HTML/ECN-7848.html</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"NASA/DFRC","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"UsageTerms":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Restrictions":{"value":"","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}}}]},"6450038":{"pageid":6450038,"ns":6,"title":"File:Model in Vortex Facility - GPN-2000-001288.jpg","index":5,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":4915609,"width":3000,"height":2400,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Model_in_Vortex_Facility_-_GPN-2000-001288.jpg/500px-Model_in_Vortex_Facility_-_GPN-2000-001288.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":500,"thumbheight":400,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Model_in_Vortex_Facility_-_GPN-2000-001288.jpg/1280px-Model_in_Vortex_Facility_-_GPN-2000-001288.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Model_in_Vortex_Facility_-_GPN-2000-001288.jpg/500px-Model_in_Vortex_Facility_-_GPN-2000-001288.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":500,"height":400,"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Model_in_Vortex_Facility_-_GPN-2000-001288.jpg/1280px-Model_in_Vortex_Facility_-_GPN-2000-001288.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/da/Model_in_Vortex_Facility_-_GPN-2000-001288.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Model_in_Vortex_Facility_-_GPN-2000-001288.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=6450038","extmetadata":{"ImageDescription":{"value":"A <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boeing_747\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:Boeing 747\">B-747</a> model has just passed through a stationary sheet of smoke, which is showing its <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wingtip_vortices\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:Wingtip vortices\">trailing vortices</a>, at the Vortex Facility at the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Langley_Research_Center\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:Langley Research Center\">Langley Research Center</a>.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" data-mw-original-href=\"http://dayton.hq.nasa.gov/IMAGES/LARGE/GPN-2000-001288.jpg\" href=\"https://dayton.hq.nasa.gov/IMAGES/LARGE/GPN-2000-001288.jpg\">Great Images in NASA</a> <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" data-mw-original-href=\"http://grin.hq.nasa.gov/ABSTRACTS/GPN-2000-001288.html\" href=\"https://grin.hq.nasa.gov/ABSTRACTS/GPN-2000-001288.html\">Description</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"NASA/LARC/photographer unknown","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"UsageTerms":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Restrictions":{"value":"","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}}}]},"78747229":{"pageid":78747229,"ns":6,"title":"File:NASA 701 Learjet 23 chase aircraft.jpg","index":10,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":948316,"width":1055,"height":703,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/NASA_701_Learjet_23_chase_aircraft.jpg/500px-NASA_701_Learjet_23_chase_aircraft.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":500,"thumbheight":333,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e3/NASA_701_Learjet_23_chase_aircraft.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/NASA_701_Learjet_23_chase_aircraft.jpg/500px-NASA_701_Learjet_23_chase_aircraft.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":500,"height":333,"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e3/NASA_701_Learjet_23_chase_aircraft.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e3/NASA_701_Learjet_23_chase_aircraft.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NASA_701_Learjet_23_chase_aircraft.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=78747229","extmetadata":{"ImageDescription":{"value":"Two chase aircraft, a Learjet 23 (NASA 701) and a Cessna T-37 (NASA/USAF 00084), are shown in formation with a Boeing B-747 jetliner (NASA 905) in this 1974 NASA Flight Research Center (FRC) photograph. The two chase aircraft were used to probe the trailing wake vortices generated by the airflow around the wings of the B-747 aircraft. The vortex trails were made visible by smoke generators mounted under the wings of the B-747 aircraft.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" data-mw-original-href=\"http://www.dfrc.nasa.gov/Gallery/Photo/B-747/HTML/ECN-4243.html\" href=\"https://www.dfrc.nasa.gov/Gallery/Photo/B-747/HTML/ECN-4243.html\">http://www.dfrc.nasa.gov/Gallery/Photo/B-747/HTML/ECN-4243.html</a> (<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" data-mw-original-href=\"http://www.dfrc.nasa.gov/Gallery/Photo/B-747/Large/ECN-4243.jpg\" href=\"https://www.dfrc.nasa.gov/Gallery/Photo/B-747/Large/ECN-4243.jpg\">direct link</a>)","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"NASA","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"UsageTerms":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Restrictions":{"value":"","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}}}]},"127183350":{"pageid":127183350,"ns":6,"title":"File:Wingtip-vortices-makeover (simple).jpg","index":3,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":491927,"width":2197,"height":1191,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Wingtip-vortices-makeover_%28simple%29.jpg/500px-Wingtip-vortices-makeover_%28simple%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":500,"thumbheight":271,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Wingtip-vortices-makeover_%28simple%29.jpg/1280px-Wingtip-vortices-makeover_%28simple%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Wingtip-vortices-makeover_%28simple%29.jpg/500px-Wingtip-vortices-makeover_%28simple%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":500,"height":271,"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d5/Wingtip-vortices-makeover_%28simple%29.jpg/1280px-Wingtip-vortices-makeover_%28simple%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/d5/Wingtip-vortices-makeover_%28simple%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wingtip-vortices-makeover_(simple).jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=127183350","extmetadata":{"ImageDescription":{"value":"<b>Wake Vortex Study at <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wallops_Flight_Facility\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:Wallops Flight Facility\">Wallops Island</a></b><br> The <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/aerodynamics\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:aerodynamics\">air flow</a> from the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/airfoil\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:airfoil\">wing</a> of this <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/crop_duster\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:crop duster\">agricultural plane</a> is made visible by a technique that uses colored smoke rising from the ground. The swirl at the wingtip traces the aircraft's <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/wake_turbulence\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:wake turbulence\">wake vortex</a>, which exerts a powerful influence on the flow field behind the plane. Because of wake vortex, the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Federal_Aviation_Administration\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:Federal Aviation Administration\">Federal Aviation Administration</a> (FAA) requires aircraft to maintain set distances behind each other when they land. A joint <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/NASA\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:NASA\">NASA</a>-FAA program aimed at boosting <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/airport\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:airport\">airport</a> capacity, however, is aimed at determining conditions under which <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/airplane\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:airplane\">planes</a> may fly closer together. NASA researchers are studying wake vortex with a variety of tools, from <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/supercomputer\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:supercomputer\">supercomputers</a>, to <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/wind_tunnel\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:wind tunnel\">wind tunnels</a>, to actual flight tests in research aircraft. Their goal is to fully understand the phenomenon, then use that knowledge to create an automated system that could predict changing wake vortex conditions at airports. <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aviator\" class=\"extiw\" title=\"en:Aviator\">Pilots</a> already know, for example, that they have to worry less about wake vortex in rough weather because windy conditions cause them to dissipate more rapidly.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Credit":{"value":"This file was derived from:  <a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Airplane_vortex_edit.jpg\" title=\"File:Airplane vortex edit.jpg\">Airplane vortex edit.jpg</a>:\u00a0<span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Airplane_vortex_edit.jpg\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Airplane_vortex_edit.jpg/60px-Airplane_vortex_edit.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" width=\"50\" height=\"41\" class=\"mw-file-element\" srcset=\"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Airplane_vortex_edit.jpg/120px-Airplane_vortex_edit.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x\" data-file-width=\"2976\" data-file-height=\"2420\"></a></span><br>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<ul><li><a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Airplane_vortex_edit.jpg\" title=\"File:Airplane vortex edit.jpg\">File:Airplane vortex edit.jpg</a>: <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" data-mw-original-href=\"http://lisar.larc.nasa.gov/\" href=\"https://lisar.larc.nasa.gov/\">NASA Langley Research Center (NASA-LaRC)</a>, Edited by <a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/User:Fir0002\" title=\"User:Fir0002\">Fir0002</a></li>\n<li>derivative work: <a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=User:MikeRun&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"User:MikeRun (page does not exist)\">MikeRun</a></li></ul>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY-SA 3.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""},"UsageTerms":{"value":"Creative Commons Attribution-Share Alike 3.0","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Restrictions":{"value":"","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}}}]}}}}